CS 499 Milestone 4 Narrative
The artifact I selected for my ePortfolio is the Android Weight Tracking application I originally developed in CS-360: Mobile Architecture and Programming. The application allows users to create an account, log in, record weight entries, establish a goal weight, and view their weight history. I selected this application again for the algorithms and data structures portion of my ePortfolio because it provides opportunities to improve how information is stored, organized, retrieved, and processed. For this enhancement, I focused on improving the way weight data is represented and using that data to calculate the user's progress toward their goal.
I included this artifact in my ePortfolio because it demonstrates my ability to work with data structures and develop algorithms that provide useful functionality to the user. The application uses a list of WeightEntry objects to manage weight history and a RecyclerView to display those entries. The database also retrieves weight entries in descending date order, which allows the application to identify the newest and oldest entries when calculating progress.
One of the main improvements was changing the weight value in the WeightEntry data structure from a formatted String into a numeric double. Previously, the application stored values in a format such as "185.5 lbs", which meant the application had to remove the " lbs" text and convert the remaining value back into a number before performing calculations. The enhancement separates the underlying data from its visual presentation. Weight values are now stored and processed numerically, while the "lbs" unit is added only when the value is displayed to the user.
I also enhanced the dashboard with an algorithm that calculates progress toward the user's goal weight. The algorithm retrieves the user's goal weight and weight history, identifies the most recent weight entry, identifies the starting weight, calculates the amount of weight lost, and then determines the percentage of progress toward the goal. I added validation for situations such as a missing goal, missing weight history, invalid weight values, and division by zero. The calculated percentage is also restricted to a logical range between zero and one hundred percent.
The planned enhancement supports the course outcome involving designing and evaluating computing solutions using algorithmic principles and computer science practices. I believe I have made substantial progress toward this outcome because I had to consider how the application's data should be represented before developing the progress calculation. Instead of treating weight as display text, I restructured the data so that it could be used directly in mathematical operations.
This enhancement also supports the outcome involving the use of well-founded techniques and tools to implement computing solutions. The application uses Java, SQLite, RecyclerView, and an object-oriented data structure to provide a functional solution. My overall outcome-coverage plan remains the same, but this enhancement gives me a stronger example of algorithmic problem solving for my ePortfolio.
One of the biggest things I learned from this enhancement was the importance of separating data from presentation. Initially, the weight value included both the number and the unit as a String. This worked for displaying information, but it created unnecessary work when the application needed to perform calculations. Changing the weight value to a numeric type made the data structure more appropriate for the problem and made the progress algorithm easier to implement and maintain.
I also learned that implementing an algorithm requires more than getting the basic calculation to work. I had to consider what would happen if the user did not have a goal, had no weight entries, entered an invalid value, or had a starting weight that would result in division by zero. These situations helped me think more defensively about how the application handles data. One challenge was making sure that the changes remained consistent across the application. Changing the weight type affected the WeightEntry class, the dashboard, the adapter, and the way values were passed between activities. This reinforced the importance of considering how changes to a data structure can affect other parts of an application.
